<section id="about" class="home-manifesto">
	<h2 id="manifesto-heading">
		Some ideas spark something in me
		<span class="accent"> instantly.</span>
		When it hits, I follow it all the way — research, iteration, obsessing over
		details — until it becomes something people will actually use.
	</h2>

	<div class="home-split">
		<div class="home-split-left">
			<p>
				I’m a people person at heart, which means I design for humans
				first and users second. I take on creative challenges and turn
				them into finished, meaningful digital products. Embracing
				the messy, imperfect reality of people. Humans aren't rational
				robots. We make mistakes, we get confused, we change our minds,
				and we love things that have character and "imperfections." When
				you design for humans first, you stop chasing sterile,
				pixel-perfect perfection that lacks soul. Instead, you design
				spaces that leave room for personality, warmth, and
				authenticity—allowing a bit of human chaos into your work
				because that’s what makes it relatable. It took me a long time
				to realize what design really is, especially since it's so often
				misunderstood. But honestly? It’s just thinking made visual.
			</p>
			<p>
				In an age dominated by AI, reminding ourselves of our
				humanity—and that you don't need to be perfect to be
				authentic—is everything to stand out.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
